The user wants a translation of Node.js code to PHP. The Node.js code reads from stdin, processes a string, and outputs a modified version based on specific casing rules.

Rules:
1. If the string is all uppercase, convert to all lowercase.
2. Else if the string has first char lowercase and rest uppercase, convert to first char uppercase and rest lowercase.
3. Else print as is.

PHP reads from stdin using `fgets(STDIN)` or `file('php://stdin')`. Since the Node code reads all input at once on 'end', I can use `file('php://stdin')` to get lines.

The Node code trims the whole input, splits by newline, maps trim. Then `readLine` consumes lines sequentially.

In PHP, I can read all lines into an array, trim them, and use an index or array_shift.

Let's write the PHP equivalent.

```php
<?php
$input = file('php://stdin', FILE_IGNORE_NEW_LINES | FILE_SKIP_EMPTY_LINES);
// The node code does .trim().split("\n").map(str => str.trim())
// file with FILE_IGNORE_NEW_LINES removes newlines. FILE_SKIP_EMPTY_LINES skips empty lines.
// But node's split("\n") on "a\nb\n" gives ["a", "b", ""] then map trim gives ["a", "b", ""].
// Then main reads first line.
// If input is just one line, it's fine.
// Let's stick to reading all lines, trimming each.
$lines = array_map('trim', file('php://stdin'));
$currentLine = 0;

function readLine() {
    global $lines, $currentLine;
    return $lines[$currentLine++];
}

function main() {
    $a = readLine();
    // Check if all uppercase
    if (strtoupper($a) === $a) {
        echo strtolower($a) . "\n";
    } 
    // Check if first char lower and rest upper
    // Node: a[0].toLowerCase() + a.substring(1).toUpperCase() === a
    // PHP: $a[0] is first char. substr($a, 1) is rest.
    // Note: $a[0] works for single-byte strings. Assuming ASCII/UTF-8 single byte chars per problem context usually.
    elseif (strtolower($a[0]) . strtoupper(substr($a, 1)) === $a) {
        echo strtoupper($a[0]) . strtolower(substr($a, 1)) . "\n";
    } 
    else {
        echo $a . "\n";
    }
}

main();
?>
```

Check edge cases: Empty string? Node `a[0]` would be undefined, `a.substring(1)` empty. `undefined.toLowerCase()` error? Actually in JS, `"".charAt(0)` is `""`, `"".substring(1)` is `""`. `"" === ""` true. But `a.toUpperCase() === a` would be true for empty string. So empty string prints empty string.
In PHP, `$a[0]` on empty string gives notice "Uninitialized string offset". `substr($a, 1)` returns false/empty.
Better to handle length check or suppress. But typical competitive programming inputs are non-empty.
I'll assume non-empty input as per standard problems (Codeforces 131A - cAPS lOCK).

The Node code uses `console.log` which adds newline. PHP `echo` needs explicit `\n`.

Final code structure.

